    ENVIRONMENTAL CONTEXT / “DOES THIS AFFECT US?”

Goal: Build enough environmental awareness to assess whether a threat, vulnerability, actor, or campaign is relevant to the agency.

Information needed:

    Hardware and software inventory
    Major applications and platforms
    Software versions where available
    Internet-facing/public-facing assets
    Critical systems and services
    Cloud environments and SaaS platforms
    Key vendors, third parties, and partnerships
    Known visibility gaps or unmanaged assets

Important note:

    CTI should use the existing authoritative asset, vulnerability, and exposure inventories where possible.
    CTI should not create a separate, competing asset inventory.

Questions:

    What systems are authoritative for asset inventory, vulnerability data, and external exposure?
    Can CTI access the information needed to determine whether a threat affects the environment?
    Who owns asset validation when CTI identifies a potentially affected technology?
    What systems/services are considered most critical or high-impact?

    THREAT REPORT TRIAGE PROCESS

Goal: Create a repeatable process for determining whether a threat report is relevant and what action should follow.

When a new threat report, advisory, vulnerability, IOC set, or actor report is identified:

    Determine whether the threat is relevant to the agency.
    Identify affected technologies, products, versions, vendors, and platforms.
    Check whether the agency has the affected technology or version.
    Check whether there is known public exposure or criticality associated with the affected asset.
    Determine whether exploitation is confirmed, suspected, or only theoretical.
    Review the threat actor, campaign, TTPs, malware, infrastructure, and initial-access methods.
    Validate IOC quality:
        Source and confidence
        Date last observed
        Context and attribution
        Whether it is likely to cause false positives
        Whether useful pivots are available
    Identify relevant CVEs, exploited products, tools, and behaviors.
    Determine whether available telemetry supports investigation.

Potential outcomes:

    Immediate escalation / urgent action
    Initial CTI investigation or threat hunt
    Vulnerability-management referral
    SOC/IR awareness and monitoring
    Pentest relevance
    Documentation only / informational awareness
    No agency relevance found

Questions:

    What specifically makes a threat report “urgent” here?
    Who needs to be notified for high-confidence, relevant threats?
    What channel should be used for urgent notifications?
    Who should receive awareness-only or informational reporting?
    Should CTI create a short assessment for every relevant report, or only high-priority items?

    SPLUNK / DEFENDER INVESTIGATION AND THREAT HUNTING

Goal: Use relevant threat intelligence to perform initial validation and hunt activity in the environment.

Potential CTI actions:

    Pivot on relevant IOCs in Splunk and Defender.
    Search for associated TTPs and behaviors, not only exact indicators.
    Check relevant log sources and telemetry coverage.
    Identify visibility gaps if the required telemetry does not exist.
    Document:
        Threat/intelligence source
        Hunt or investigation hypothesis
        Scope and time period
        Searches performed
        Findings
        Limitations or telemetry gaps
        Recommended next actions

Important point:

    “No results found” should not automatically mean the agency was not affected.
    It may mean the data does not exist, telemetry was not retained, the search was too narrow, or the IOC was not useful.

Potential output:

    Preliminary Threat Hunt / Threat Relevance Assessment
        What happened externally
        Why it may matter to the agency
        Systems or technologies potentially affected
        Searches/hunts performed
        Findings
        Gaps and limitations
        Recommended follow-up

Questions:

    Does CTI have authority to run these searches independently?
    When should CTI hand a finding to IR or the SOC?
    Is there an existing threat-hunting process or template?
    Who owns detection engineering if a hunt identifies a detection gap?
    How should hunts be tracked and prioritized?

    GOOGLE THREAT INTELLIGENCE

Potential uses:

    Monitor threat reporting relevant to agency technologies, vendors, and infrastructure.
    Enrich indicators and suspicious artifacts.
    Identify threat actor, malware, campaign, and infrastructure context.
    Monitor agency domains and lookalike/impersonation domains.
    Monitor potentially exposed credentials or references to agency infrastructure.
    Support research into publicly reported vulnerabilities, tooling, and active exploitation.
    Build technology, actor, and threat watchlists relevant to the environment.

Potential integration work:

    Evaluate the Google Threat Intelligence / Splunk integration.
    Explore automated enrichment and IOC correlation.
    Identify whether useful matches can be routed into an analyst-review workflow.
    Reduce manual IOC lookups and repetitive enrichment tasks.

Guardrails:

    Do not automatically block indicators based solely on external intelligence.
    Confirm licensing, technical compatibility, approved data flows, privacy rules, and engineering ownership before implementing integrations.
    Keep executive monitoring limited to approved work-related impersonation, exposure, or security-risk use cases.

Questions:

    What Google Threat Intelligence capabilities and licenses are available?
    Is there an existing Splunk integration or add-on?
    Who in Security Engineering owns integrations and automation?
    What monitoring use cases are highest value right now?
    What approvals are required before creating new monitoring or automated workflows?

    VULNERABILITY MANAGEMENT / VSMT / CDM SUPPORT

Goal: Help prioritize vulnerabilities based on real-world threat context and agency relevance.

Potential CTI contributions:

    Identify confirmed active exploitation.
    Identify exploitation by relevant threat actors or ransomware groups.
    Identify public exploit availability or proof-of-concept maturity.
    Identify affected products, versions, and technologies in the environment.
    Provide context on likely impact, initial-access methods, and relevant TTPs.
    Help distinguish “high CVSS” from “high operational risk to us.”
    Identify where asset inventory, exposure data, or telemetry gaps make prioritization difficult.

Potential output:

    Threat-Informed Vulnerability Assessment
        Vulnerability/CVE
        Exploitation status
        Threat actor or campaign relevance
        Affected agency technologies
        Internet exposure / asset criticality
        Recommended priority
        Recommended validation or mitigation actions

Questions:

    What information does VSMT need from CTI to change vulnerability priority?
    Who confirms whether affected technologies exist in the environment?
    Who makes the final remediation-priority decision?
    What is the escalation path for actively exploited vulnerabilities?
    How does CTI receive confirmation that an issue was remediated or accepted as risk?

    INCIDENT RESPONSE SUPPORT

Goal: Provide intelligence support during investigations and major incidents.

Potential CTI support during incidents:

    Relevant threat actor and campaign context
    Related IOCs, domains, IPs, file hashes, and infrastructure
    TTP mapping and likely next actions
    Threat actor motivations and targeting patterns
    Malware/tooling analysis and known behaviors
    Victimology and sector targeting
    Related vulnerabilities or initial-access methods
    Hunt leads and investigative pivots
    Executive-ready intelligence summaries

Questions:

    At what incident severity is CTI engaged?
    Who requests CTI support during an incident?
    What should CTI prioritize during surge support?
    What routine CTI work pauses during a major incident?
    Is CTI only providing intelligence support, or expected to assist with hands-on response work as well?

    PENTESTING SUPPORT

Goal: Make pentesting more threat-informed and relevant to realistic adversary activity.

Potential pre-engagement CTI support:

    Review the authorized engagement scope.
    Identify relevant systems, technologies, public exposure, and known vulnerabilities.
    Identify 1–2 threat actors, campaigns, or threat themes relevant to the target environment.
    Map relevant behaviors to MITRE ATT&CK.
    Identify known initial-access techniques, tools, vulnerabilities, and tradecraft.
    Produce a concise threat-informed engagement brief.

Potential output:

    Pentest Threat Intelligence Brief
        Environment/technology context
        Relevant threat actors or campaigns
        MITRE ATT&CK techniques to emulate or consider
        Known vulnerabilities and exposure themes
        Initial-access methods
        Relevant tooling or malware behavior
        Detection opportunities for defenders

Future concept:

    Explore a more formal red-team capability later.
    Do not treat this as an immediate commitment; it would require separate scope, staffing, authorities, and planning.

Questions:

    How often does the pentest team need CTI support?
    What deliverable would actually be useful before an engagement?
    What information should feed back from pentest findings into CTI, detection engineering, vulnerability management, and threat hunting?

